      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" aria-expanded="false" onclick="this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',this.getAttribute('aria-expanded')==='true'?'false':'true');this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('open')">
          What's the best time of year for a roof inspection in Tampa Bay?
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1A1A1A" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <div class="faq-answer-inner">The single best time in Florida is <strong>spring, specifically March through May</strong>, before hurricane season starts June 1. Weather is mild, materials aren't heat-softened, and you have time to complete any repairs before the first tropical system arrives. Full seasonal breakdown: <strong>Spring (Mar-May)</strong> is the top-priority window, annual professional inspection plus any needed repairs. <strong>Summer (Jun-Aug)</strong> is inspection-only-after-storms: surface temps exceed 150&deg;F on afternoon roofs and sealants cure poorly. If a storm hits, get inspected within 5 days for insurance-claim timelines. <strong>Fall (Sep-Nov)</strong> is post-hurricane assessment: cumulative wind stress causes hidden seam separations and underlayment damage even without a direct hit. <strong>Winter (Dec-Feb)</strong> is repair-and-renewal season: the driest weather in Tampa Bay, plus most FL homeowner policies renew in this window and a fresh inspection is often required for wind-mitigation credits. Rule of thumb: schedule the annual inspection in spring, add a second in fall if any tropical system passed within 200 miles, and never skip a post-storm inspection.</div>
